  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 155 PM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon shows low pressure over \nnorthern Ontario with a cold front that extended SW to Indiana and \nsouthern IL. Radar shows convection developing ahead of the front \nover southern Central Indiana, quickly moving SE. Southwest flow was \nin place ahead of the front across southern Indiana, while flow \nshifted to the west or NW across the northern half of the state. \n\nLate this afternoon, convection ahead of the front is expected to \ncontinue across the southern parts of the forecast area and exit by \n20Z or earlier. Storms may produce heavy rain and gusty winds within \nthis moisture rich air mass and marginal dynamics in place.\n\nTonight through Thursday...\n\nQuiet and cooler weather will settle across Central Indiana starting \ntonight and continue through at least Thursday. This will be due to \nstrong upper ridging building across the western CONUS and the \nresultant NW flow and lee-side subsidence across the upper midwest \nspilling across Indiana. Models suggest a strong and large area of \nhigh pressure building across Indiana through Thursday, along with \ncooler north or northeast flow. This Canadian high will provide \nmostly sunny days and mostly clear nights through Thursday. Below \nnormal temperatures will be expected with highs in the middle 70s on \nWednesday before rising to around 80 on Thursday.\n\nFriday through Tuesday...\n\nDry weather with a warming trend looks in store for Friday through \nSunday, while minimal, uncertain rain chances return on Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nDuring this period, models continue to suggest strong ridging aloft \nbuilding over the western CONUS. This will result in continued \nnorthwest flow and lee side subsidence forming over the Upper \nmidwest, spilling southeast to Indiana and the Ohio Valley. This \nwill result in a large area of high pressure settling and lingering \nacross Indiana and the Ohio Valley through Sunday. Northeasterly \nwinds on Friday and Saturday will allow cooler temperatures to spill \ninto Central Indiana, resulting in below normal temperatures through \nthe first part of the weekend.\n\nBy Sunday, reorganization of the high pressure systems east of \nIndiana will allow for warmer, southerly surface flow to return to \nCentral Indiana. This will allow highs to return to the middle and \nupper 80s for Sunday.\n\nYesterday/s runs suggested weak waves moving across Indiana within \nthe flow aloft on Sunday and Monday, which could provide forcing for \nrain. However today, models have shifted these features farther east \nand slower. Models now suggest Indiana will continue to be protected \nby ridging aloft on Monday and NW flow on Tuesday. Confidence for \nrain during those times remains low until a stronger and more \nsignificant signal appears.",
